# Two ways to broaden which source IPs may reach the API and chat UI beyond
# localhost. Either set EULLM_ALLOWED_IPS in the process environment, or copy
# this file to .env in the directory you launch `eullm` from. The environment
# takes precedence when both are set.
#
#   docker run -e EULLM_ALLOWED_IPS=192.168.1.0/24 ...
#   systemd:  Environment=EULLM_ALLOWED_IPS=192.168.1.0/24
#
# With neither set — or no EULLM_ALLOWED_IPS key in the file — only 127.0.0.1
# and ::1 (loopback) are allowed, regardless of the server binding 0.0.0.0.
# The startup log reports which source was actually used, so you can confirm
# your configuration was picked up.
#
# NOTE for Docker: with published ports (-p 11434:11434) external clients
# reach the process as the bridge gateway address, not their own IP, so an
# allowlist cannot distinguish them from each other. Treat this as a
# convenience boundary for a trusted LAN, not as access control on an
# untrusted network.
# Loopback always stays allowed even when you add entries below; you can't
# lock yourself out of local access by configuring a remote host.
#
# Comma-separated list of bare IPs and/or CIDR subnets. Examples:
#
#   A single RAG host calling the engine from another machine:
#     EULLM_ALLOWED_IPS=203.0.113.5
#
#   A whole LAN subnet (any device on 192.168.1.0/24):
#     EULLM_ALLOWED_IPS=192.168.1.0/24
#
#   Both, plus an IPv6 host:
#     EULLM_ALLOWED_IPS=203.0.113.5,192.168.1.0/24,2001:db8::1

EULLM_ALLOWED_IPS=

# ── API keys ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set this and the API requires a bearer token; leave it empty and there is no
# authentication (the IP allowlist above is then the only control). This is the
# control that works behind Docker's published ports, precisely because it
# identifies the caller rather than the route the packet took.
#
#   EULLM_API_KEYS=ci:8f3b1d9c2e7a4f60b5,rag-prod:1a2b3c4d5e6f7a8b9c:rpm=600
#
# Format: id:secret[:rpm=N], comma-separated. The id is not a secret — it is
# written into every audit record's user_id, which is what makes the AI Act
# trail attributable. The secret must be at least 16 characters. rpm caps
# requests per minute for that key (omitted or 0 = unlimited).
#
# A valid key admits a request from ANY source address: enabling keys replaces
# address-based admission with identity-based admission instead of stacking on
# top of it. Requests with no key or a wrong key get 401 whatever their origin,
# loopback included.
#
# An entry that does not parse is fatal at startup: if you asked for
# authentication, serving without it because of a typo is worse than not
# starting.
#
# For real deployments prefer a file — it can be chmod 600, whereas an
# environment variable is readable through /proc/<pid>/environ and tends to end
# up in logs. One id:secret[:rpm=N] per line, # comments allowed:
#
#   EULLM_API_KEYS_FILE=/etc/eullm/keys

EULLM_API_KEYS=

# ── Browser origins ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Which browser origins may call the API. With nothing set, any loopback origin
# is allowed on any port (so the bundled chat UI and a local frontend work) and
# a cross-origin request with side effects is refused with 403. Requests with no
# Origin header at all — curl, an Ollama SDK, a RAG pipeline — are unaffected.
#
#   EULLM_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://chat.example.eu,http://192.168.7.10:8080
#
# `*` restores the fully permissive behaviour, explicitly.

EULLM_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# ── Web tool (--web) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A URL in a prompt is fetched by the server, so on a shared deployment it is
# attacker-controlled. By default only https is fetched, and only from hosts
# that resolve to public addresses.
#
#   EULLM_WEB_ALLOWED_DOMAINS=docs.example.eu,eur-lex.europa.eu
#   EULLM_WEB_ALLOW_HTTP=1
#
# The next one turns the address check off, for an intranet documentation server
# that is the whole point of the deployment. With it set, whoever writes the
# prompt has a GET primitive on your internal network.
#
#   EULLM_WEB_ALLOW_PRIVATE_HOSTS=1

# ── Model paths in requests ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Off by default: a request's `model` field resolves inside the model store or a
# mounted /models directory, not to arbitrary filesystem paths. Set this only
# for local use.
#
#   EULLM_ALLOW_MODEL_PATHS=1
